course: dictionary
lesson: 12289
title: Converse: talk, opposite, and if-then
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To converse means to talk with someone. It is a formal or old-fashioned word, so you will mostly find it in books. «The two old friends conversed for hours by the fire.» They were not just saying a few words; they were chatting together for a long time.
explain: Converse can also be a noun, meaning the opposite or the reverse of something. «Hot is the converse of cold.» The word tells you the two things are opposites, pointing in different directions.
explain: In maths and logic, the converse swaps the two parts of an «if… then» sentence. «If it is raining, the ground is wet» becomes «If the ground is wet, it is raining» when you take its converse. Be careful: a true statement's converse is not always true, because the ground could be wet from a sprinkler.
fact: Converse can be a verb, a noun or an adjective.
fact: As a verb, «converse with» means to talk with someone: «She conversed with her teacher after class.»
fact: As a noun, «the converse» means the opposite or the reverse: «Up is the converse of down.»
fact: The converse of «if A, then B» is «if B, then A».
fact: The converse of a true statement is not always true: «If it is a square, it has four sides» is true, but «If it has four sides, it is a square» is false, because a rectangle has four sides too.
exercise: «The two neighbours conversed over the garden fence.» What does conversed mean? | Threw a ball | Argued loudly | Ran away | *Talked together
exercise: Ella says «The converse of hot is cold.» What does converse mean here? | A loud noise | *The opposite | The same thing | The very start
exercise: The statement is «If it is Sunday, the shops are closed.» What is its converse? | Shops are closed on Sundays | If it is Sunday, the shops are open | *If the shops are closed, it is Sunday | The shops are open on Monday
test: «They conversed quietly in the library.» Which word means about the same as conversed here? | *talked | ran | slept | shouted
test: What is the converse of «if I see lightning, I hear thunder»? | *if I hear thunder, I see lightning | if I see lightning, I hear thunder | if I do not hear thunder, I see no lightning
exam: Is the converse of «if a shape is a square, it has four equal sides» always true? | Yes, because squares are shapes | *No, a rhombus has four equal sides but is not a square | Yes, the converse is always true | No, because squares have three sides
